.TH CCOL_VERSION 3 "2026-11-09" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_version, ccol_version_string \- version of the library a program
runs with
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

#define CCOL_VERSION_MAJOR  2
#define CCOL_VERSION_MINOR  0
#define CCOL_VERSION_PATCH  0
#define CCOL_VERSION_NUMBER /* 0xMMmmpp, here 0x020000 */
#define CCOL_VERSION_STRING /* "MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH", here "2.0.0" */

unsigned int ccol_version(void);
const char *ccol_version_string(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The macros give the release of the headers that a program is compiled
with. The two functions give the release of the shared library that the
dynamic loader picked when the program started. The two can differ: a
program built against 2.0.0 runs with every later 2.x release, because
they all have the SONAME libccollections.so.2.

CCOL_VERSION_NUMBER packs the three numbers as
(MAJOR << 16) | (MINOR << 8) | PATCH, so two releases compare as plain
integers. MINOR and PATCH are each below 256.

ccol_version() returns the packed number of the loaded library, in the
same form as CCOL_VERSION_NUMBER, and ccol_version_string() its text in
the same form as CCOL_VERSION_STRING.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_version() returns the packed number. ccol_version_string() returns a
pointer to a string with static storage duration, which never changes
and must not be freed. Neither function fails.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Thread-safe. Neither function reads or writes any state.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ccollections/common.h>

int main(void) {
  /* A feature that came with 2.1 can be tested at run time. */
  if (ccol_version() < 0x020100u)
    printf("library %s is older than 2.1\en", ccol_version_string());
  printf("built with %s, running with %s\en", CCOL_VERSION_STRING,
         ccol_version_string());
  return 0;
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccollections (7)
